= v_P, 0 ≤ p_i ≤ v_i}, because the pair and singleton core constraints collapse onto those bounds.\n- **Change of coordinates:** in slack coordinates q = v − p, F is a slice of a box whose far corner is the VCG point, at m_i = min(v_i, s) with s = S − v_P.\n- **The Vickrey-nearest point:** it is the projection of m onto F, which is m − τ·1 (τ = (Σm − s)/3) unless some coordinate is pushed to zero. If a coordinate hits zero, it can't equal the centroid, which lies strictly inside the polygon.\n- **The centroid:** F is a triangle whose centroid is m − τ·1, with up to three non-overlapping corners cut off. Cut-off ratios r_i give the closed form g − G ∝ −Σ r_i²(1 − r_i)(1 − 3e_i). This is zero exactly when the weights r_i²(1 − r_i) are equal. Since that function is strictly increasing on the relevant range, this means either no corners are cut (case 1) or all m_i are equal (cases 2 and 3).\n\n**Example where they differ:** v = (1, 2, 3), v_P = 3. VCG is (0, 0, 0) and F is the parallelogram with corners (0,0,3), (1,0,2), (1,2,0), (0,2,1).\n- The centroid is (1/2, 1, 3/2), which is proportional to the values.\n- The Vickrey-nearest point is (1, 1, 1), an equal split.\n\nThe file also has a second example where the two differ and a value cap binds, plus one example each for cases 1 and 2 where they coincide.\n\n**Checks:** with exact arithmetic I computed both points for every integer v in {1..9}³ with every integer v_P < S, and for 30,000 random rational instances. They coincided in exactly the cases the condition predicts, with no exceptions.","ttft_ms":2220,"type":"result","duration_ms":147880,"uuid":"d8624863-81c4-4835-9d99-84e6bdf25be8","ttft_stream_ms":1950,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":1950,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
